WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.510
- I thought that scientists
understood how explosions work.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.090
Now, we're not going to
see big orange fireballs

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.020
going up into the sky.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.210
I've done that before, I've
filmed with a pyrotechnician.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.950
We added a load of fuel

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.680
to make the blast look
like a Hollywood explosion.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.540
But that is not realistic.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.940
Besides, these days
Hollywood, and scientists,

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.600
can simulate those sort of effects
near-perfectly in a computer.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.433
I looked it up.

00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.650
There are industry standard
ways of predicting blast waves.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.160
They are very accurate...
once you're about half a meter away

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.990
and there's clear air in between,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.840
which is good enough for most
people, 'cause let's face it,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.390
if you're five centimeters
away from an explosion,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.430
you've probably got bigger problems.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.280
But it turns out that's big picture stuff.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.080
Right at the start, in the detonation,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.220
in the first few instants of an explosion
as all that energy is released,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.320
that's where the simulations break down,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.960
and solving that problem could save lives.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.330
This bleak old World War II munitions site
is now a university test laboratory

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.850
and the research is close up.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.680
So let's see a test.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.380
- Explosions are very
difficult to measure.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.030
We need something that's
going to be rugged

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.460
and strong enough to survive an explosion,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.710
but we also need something
that's sensitive enough

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.870
to measure things that are changing

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:17.130
over millimeter length scales
and sub-microsecond timescales.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.860
The rig that we're going to
be using today is called MaCE,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.430
which stands for "Mechanisms
and Characterization of Explosions".

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.780
It's a bit of world-leading kit

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.310
where we can directly measure much closer
than anybody worldwide can do,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.140
gigapascals worth of pressures,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.450
and that's a very, very
complicated situation

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.700
which we can't necessarily
model or predict currently.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.350
And we're going to use some
high speed video cameras

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.540
to film what's going on as well.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.730
- If you hit a metal bar the right way,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.250
it'll make a ringing sound,
basically a musical note.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.980
That ringing is made by
the bar itself vibrating

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.690
as the force from that initial hit

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.030
bounces back and forth within the metal.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.340
And the pitch of that note
is really easy to measure.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.480
You just listen.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.920
The work the researchers are doing here

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.530
is much more technical
and much more precise,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.480
but it's kind of the same process.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.790
- It's based on theory
by a British physicist

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.090
called Bertram Hopkinson,
109 years ago,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.790
and it's only now that the
technology is catching up

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.730
where we can affix
very, very small strain gauges

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.980
and we've got data acquisition
systems that can sample it

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.810
at the required rates and
required frequencies.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.300
The strain gauge is a tiny dot

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.330
that we mount on the outside
of Hopkinson pressure bars,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.287
solid steel, one meter long,
four millimeter diameter.

00:02:25.287 --> 00:02:30.540
And we fix this strain gauge 40mm
from the loaded face of the bar,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.200
We sample it 5 million
times per second.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.910
We're going to set the explosive

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.400
a little bit further
away from the surface,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.740
so hopefully we can see the fireball

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.530
and the detonation
products start to expand.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.360
That's going to set up a blast wave

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.570
that's going to propagate
through the bar as a stress wave.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.960
So the strain gauge records
a change in voltage.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.780
We can work out stress, which
we can work out the pressure

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.430
and the load on the end of the bar from.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.263
And there's a whacking
great big steel plate

00:02:59.263 --> 00:03:02.970
that just acts as a shield
and just deflects everything away.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.590
So the pressure bars trap that pressure

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.330
and send it downwards,
and then the arrangement

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.130
of the MaCE rig that
we're building towards

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.010
is 37 of these measurement locations.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.670
But today we've just got one!
Just to focus on what's happening

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.500
right in the most severe part.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.830
- So I asked the researchers,
what's the use for this?

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.500
Their first answer was, well, for science,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.670
and that's fair enough,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.410
but there are practical applications.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.330
To start with, if this works,

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:32.160
building things that resist explosions
will become a lot easier and cheaper.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.630
- In the 80s and the 90s,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:38.760
the big challenge was big truck bombs
with tonnes of explosives.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.850
Nowadays, explosive attacks
are much more targeted.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.330
They tend to be smaller devices,
and as a result,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.390
they're located closer to the thing
that they want to damage.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.110
So that's why we've looked
in this very extreme near-field setting.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.250
We're going to be doing some tests today
with 100 grams of high explosive.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.710
It's around about tennis ball sized.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.110
There are a lot of secondary
reactions that go on.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.840
It takes a while to develop,
at least on our time scales,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.970
and there are ongoing chemical reactions.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.880
And that offers up the really
interesting prospect of,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.680
can we do something that's
going to mitigate them

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.090
or even turn them off?

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.660
Can we surround explosives in
a particular type of material

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.400
to manage those reactions?

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.900
Bombproof luggage containers
or storage containers on transport?

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.744
That's a potential thing that could
be done with this research.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.630
- If we can understand
those first instants of an explosion,

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.580
then maybe we can help
keep it under control.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.860
After all, this munitions
site is built to do that.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.346
It was designed to contain
anything that goes wrong by brute force,

00:04:38.346 --> 00:04:41.820
just using huge amounts of earth
and concrete bunkers.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.960
So can that be done with
less materials and less cost?

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.117
We're about to take just one
step towards finding out.

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:52.380
[horn blaring]

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.170
- We've never been able
to observe these features before,

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.140
so we kind of don't know
what we don't know.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.370
That's what the group looks at,

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.953
uncovering what we don't
know about explosions yet.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.130
- All right, what have we got?

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:15.660
- So this is filmed at about
a quarter of a million frames per second.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.533
- Wow, okay.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:22.293
Wow.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.863
You can see it bounce back!

00:05:27.747 --> 00:05:30.240
- So it hits the surface.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.330
And then as it goes back up,
see it reigniting.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.580
All that hot gas is
getting churned back up

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.050
and it's mixing with the oxygen.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.970
And that's giving that secondary kick

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.100
that we see at these
sorts of scale distances.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.020
It's continually pushing
the end of that bar

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.450
and it's sending that information down

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:46.620
- Because it's not one hit.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.000
It's a push that-

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.550
It feels like one hit.
It feels like one hit.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.383
- Exactly.

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:54.060
- But you are recording that so quickly

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.370
that what you're actually
getting is a push.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:57.963
- Yep.
- Wow.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:05:59.813
- Cool, isn't it?

00:05:59.813 --> 00:06:01.276
- That's pretty cool.

